\begin{frame}{Introduction to Stochastic Process}
       A stochastic process is a mathematical model that describes how a random variable changes over time (or space). In biological context, you can definite it as the process whose future behaviour is not completely predictable because it involves randomness.
       \begin{itemize}
           \item     A stochastic process is a collection of random variables $\{~x(t), t\in T~\}$ where, $x(t)$ is an \textbf{random variables} representing the state of the function at \textbf{time} $t$.\\[2pt]



              \item  In addition, a stochastic process may be collection of random vectors. For examples, two random variables is a collection of random vectors $\{(X(t), Y(t)), t\in T\}$. % I will add examples for defn  
              
       \end{itemize}
\end{frame}


\begin{frame}{Reaction Diffusion Equation}
    Chemotaxis is the directed movement of cells in response to a chemical concentration gradient. Unlike diffusion, where particles move from a region of higher concentration to lower concentration, chemotaxis allows cells to move up the concentration gradient, i.e., towards a higher concentration of a chemical attractant. This directional movement enables cells to locate food sources, sites of infection, or other biologically important signals.
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Directed movement due to chemical signals.
        \item Movement occurs towards increasing attractant concentration.
        \item Different from diffusion, which is random and moves down the concentration gradient.
    \end{itemize}
\end{frame}

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\section{Random Walk}

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\subsection{Definition}

\begin{frame}{Random Walk}

\begin{block}{Definition}
A \textbf{random walk} is a stochastic process in which a particle (or animal) moves in discrete steps. At every time interval $\tau$, the particle takes a step of fixed length $\delta$ either to the left or to the right with equal probability.
\end{block}

\vspace{0.3cm}

\begin{columns}

\column{0.55\textwidth}

\begin{itemize}
    \item One-dimensional motion.
    \item Initial position:
    \[
    x(0)=0.
    \]
    \item Step length:
    \[
    \delta
    \]
    \item Time per step:
    \[
    \tau
    \]
    \item Equal probability of moving in either direction:
    \[
    P(+\delta)=\frac12,\qquad
    P(-\delta)=\frac12.
    \]
\end{itemize}

